i'm dave i run a surfboard repair shop  in la and today i'm going to run you  through how to restore a vintage dewy  weber surfboard  to start i clean the board using a razor  blade and acetone  you don't want to use a brand new razor  blade just because they're so sharp  sometimes they will scratch the surface  of the board underneath the wax when  you're getting that off and cleaning it  up  from what i can tell from this board i  think it was probably made in the 1960s  when restoring a vintage board it's  definitely important to  take note of any original markings which  include a signature by the shaper any  kind of logos from when it was  originally made you want to keep all  that intact  because it definitely affects the  authenticity of the surfboard  [Music]  the delamination occurs when heat or  stress or anything else separates the  fiberglass from the foam core of the  surfboard technique for delaminations  it's pretty easy all kind of knock on  the board  if it sounds like you're hitting a  countertop that's usually a good sound  it sounds kind of like a dual thud or  like cardboard that's a sure sign that  it's de-animated if the fiberglass isn't  adhered well to the foam underneath  there's a lot of flexing happening if  you let's say falling away or drop in  late on the wave and your feet land  where a delamination is you're much more  likely to snap the board in half  next i'll check the board for dings  dings are any any crack in the  fiberglass of the surfboard i determine  what tool i'm going to use based on the  severity of the ding  but i generally start with any kind of  abrasive or sanding tool usually a  grinding disc or some kind of sandpaper  fourth step is  generally filling any dings that are  dented all the way into the foam of the  surfboard  the filled dings i use q cell and  laminating resin i generally prefer to  mix a lot of q gel into the resin and  get it almost consistency of peanut  butter or thicker the only dings that  don't require filling is where the  integrity of the shape  or the outline of the surfboard isn't  changed  so this board looks like it had been  previously repaired someone attempted to  repair this delamination before that's  what all these spots are from the top of  the board and i pulled that flap up all  that resin pulled out of the foam but it  stayed adhered to  the flap of fiberglass  i'm removing these hanging pieces of  resin because uh i'm using filler on the  board and that's going to build those  holes so it won't fit back in  i'm mixing up resin and hue cells this  is forming a kind of paste and when it  cures it forms a chemical bond between  that flap of fiberglass and foam core of  the surfboard so i'm applying this in  between those two layers  [Music]  i'm i'm kind of getting rid of any of uh  the foam that's stuck to this panel of  fiberglass and what that's going to do  is enable that fiberglass to lay uh more  flush against the foam pore of the  support  so right here i'm using egg crate foam  i'm putting that on the board because  the next step we're going to lay some  wood over the top of the surfboard and  then use clamps to uh to fasten that  duct and that adheres this flap back to  the surface of the phone  [Music]  what i'm doing now is sanding down some  of this resin that squeezed through the  cracks when i clamped the flap down  a filler that i used isn't necessarily  waterproof it's actually porous and so  fiberglass and resin once applied to the  fiberglass creates a waterproof barrier  this goes over your filler in this case  we're not going to do fiberglass over  the entire top of the board because that  would make it too heavy  it generally takes between one and three  hours for your resin to cure whether  it's a hot coat or lamination you can  speed it up by adding more resin but  you're gonna get not a very good cure so  you gotta stick to the ratios  six step sanding or feathering the edges  of that fiberglass back down into the  regular board  the purpose of feathering your  fiberglass patches is aesthetic as well  as functional if you have a hard square  patch that could cause or to fracture  along that flat edge if it's gliding  after that would be applying your hot  coat resin as some people call it a hot  coat is laminating resin mixed with a  chemical additive called surfacing agent  and what that is is wax it rises to the  top of the resin as it sets and it  allows you to stand at the same paper on  the top of the board the damage is so  extensive that i'm just going to do one  hot coat over the entire surfboard  [Music]  on the bottom the damage looks a lot  less extensive so i'm just going to  focus on the specific areas that need  attention rather than spending the time  doing the entire bottom of the board  the eighth step is  sanding those areas that you were  working on  i've used a 60 grit sanding disc that's  the lowest or most coarse sandpaper i  use when i'm grinding my lamination  after doing my hot coat i'm actually  doing the fine sanding start with 120  grit move to 220  350.  start wet sanding at 400 then up to 600  and then  generally you'd be done at 600 for a  sanded finish  but if you're doing a polished finish  like this you go from 600 to 800  to 1200 this board took me about three  hours to sand that's a pretty standard  amount of time for a job like this  skipping grits would save time but you  would not come out with a good finish  it's important to spend the time on each  grit if you want your board to come out  looking for steam  it's time to polish the board so you  grab your polishing compound and a  buffing wheel and polish the board up  polishing the board it's purely  aesthetic that's how you're going to get  that mirror finish on surfboard  dewey weber was one of the most famous  uh and influential surfboard shapers of  his time when i first got the board i  paid around 300 for it  [Music]  in its restored condition i would  estimate this board's worth somewhere  around a thousand dollars  this restoration was  super satisfying and fun to work on i'm  really excited to get this board back  out and go surf  now the board is fixed it'll last a  lifetime if taken care of correctly  [Music]
 

